From 57d8ecde57eef613b023412a0409d4ca5c4d3bf7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jacob Root <me@rooty.dev>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 13:31:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 10/11] vm_control: prefault virtio-nvgpu's window mappings

A guest's first touch of each page of a window mapping faulted out to KVM,
about 3.1 us a page for BAR1 video memory (a PFNMAP): a fresh 64 MiB
mapping was written at 1.3 GB/s against the host's 11. KVM_PRE_FAULT_MEMORY
(Linux 6.11) maps the same pages from inside the kernel, about 0.13 us a
page, but it is a vCPU's call and a running vCPU holds its lock in KVM_RUN.

So the main process makes one more vCPU that never runs, and a thread of
its own prefaults each window mapping the nvgpu tube registered, after the
mapping has been answered: a hint bounded to 1024 waiting ranges. The vCPU
is made only for a VM with an nvgpu device and only where the VM reports
the call (Vm::has_pre_fault_memory, KVM_CAP_PRE_FAULT_MEMORY), before the
guest's vCPUs, with the supported CPUID (so the same TDP root) and the id
prefault_vcpu_id gives it: one past the largest guest vCPU id. Those ids
are the APIC ids the MADT lists -- 0..n-1, or the host CPUs' APIC ids with
--host-cpu-topology and per-vCPU affinity -- so the spare's is in no ACPI
table and collides with no guest vCPU's. A range withdrawn before its turn
is refused by KVM and passed over; ENOSYS, ENOTTY or EOPNOTSUPP (no such
call, or none for this VM: shadow paging, a protected VM) stops the thread
for good, said once.

